       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 30 mg is three times the usual maximum daily dose of 10 mg. The FDA-labeled range for adults is 2.5 to 10 mg once daily. Doses above 10 mg raise the risk of severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and peripheral edema, and overdose can cause prolonged vasodilatory shock. Your patient's normal kidney and liver function and lack of allergies don't change that limit.\n\nWhich of these did you intend?\n\n- **10 mg by mouth once daily**, the standard maximum.\n- **5 mg by mouth once daily**, a typical starting or maintenance dose. The starting dose is 2.5 mg for frail or elderly patients, or 5 mg for most adults.\n- **Another dose.** If 30 mg is deliberate, for example as part of a specific protocol, tell me and I'll add a note documenting the rationale. I'd still suggest confirming with pharmacy first.\n\nIf the goal is more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives, adding a second agent such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide is usually preferred to going above the maximum.\n\nTell me which dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
